{
  "topic": "g11-image-representation",
  "summary": "How bitmap images are stored as pixels, what resolution and colour depth mean and how they affect quality and file size, how to calculate the size of a bitmap image file in bits, bytes, kilobytes and megabytes, and how vector images differ.",
  "theory": [
    {
      "title": "Bitmap images and pixels",
      "objectives": [
        "11.1.1.5"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "definition",
          "term": "Bitmap (raster) image",
          "content": "An image made of a **grid of tiny squares called pixels** (picture elements). Each pixel has **one colour**, stored as a **binary number**. Photos from cameras and scanned images are bitmaps (BMP, PNG, JPEG, GIF)."
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "format": "ascii",
          "content": "  0 1 1 1 1 0        . # # # # .\n  1 0 0 0 0 1        # . . . . #\n  1 0 1 1 0 1   →    # . # # . #\n  1 0 0 0 0 1        # . . . . #\n  0 1 1 1 1 0        . # # # # .",
          "caption": "A 6 × 5 black-and-white bitmap (1 bit per pixel: 1 = black, 0 = white)"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "The computer stores the image as a sequence of binary codes, one for each pixel, plus **metadata** (width, height, colour depth, file type) so that it can be displayed correctly."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Resolution",
      "objectives": [
        "11.1.1.5"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "definition",
          "term": "Image resolution",
          "content": "The **number of pixels** in an image, usually given as **width × height** (e.g. **1920 × 1080**). It can also be given as the total number of pixels (2 073 600 ≈ 2 megapixels)."
        },
        {
          "type": "cards",
          "items": [
            {
              "title": "Higher resolution",
              "content": "- **More pixels** → more **detail**; the image stays sharp when enlarged or printed.\n- **Larger file size** — more pixels must be stored."
            },
            {
              "title": "Lower resolution",
              "content": "- **Fewer pixels** → less detail; enlarging makes the image **pixelated** (blocky).\n- **Smaller file** — faster to load and send."
            },
            {
              "title": "Screen and print resolution",
              "content": "- **PPI / DPI** (pixels or dots **per inch**) shows how densely pixels are displayed or printed. A 300 PPI print looks sharper than a 72 PPI one."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Colour depth",
      "objectives": [
        "11.1.1.5"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "definition",
          "term": "Colour depth (bit depth)",
          "content": "The **number of bits used to store the colour of each pixel**. With a colour depth of *n* bits, **2ⁿ different colours** can be represented."
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "Colour depth and number of colours",
          "columns": [
            "Colour depth",
            "Number of colours",
            "Typical use"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1 bit",
              "2¹ = **2**",
              "Black and white"
            ],
            [
              "4 bits",
              "2⁴ = **16**",
              "Simple icons"
            ],
            [
              "8 bits",
              "2⁸ = **256**",
              "GIF images, simple graphics"
            ],
            [
              "16 bits",
              "2¹⁶ = **65 536**",
              "\"High colour\""
            ],
            [
              "24 bits",
              "2²⁴ = **16 777 216**",
              "\"True colour\" photos — 8 bits each for **red, green, blue**"
            ],
            [
              "32 bits",
              "2²⁴ colours + 8 bits **transparency** (alpha)",
              "PNG images with transparency"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "exam_tip",
          "content": "Find the smallest *n* with 2ⁿ ≥ the number of colours: **5 colours** need 3 bits (2³ = 8); **100 colours** need 7 bits (2⁷ = 128); **256 colours** need exactly 8 bits."
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "Effect of colour depth and resolution",
          "columns": [
            "Change",
            "Image quality",
            "File size"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Increase **resolution**",
              "More detail, sharper",
              "**Increases** (in proportion to the number of pixels)"
            ],
            [
              "Increase **colour depth**",
              "More colours, smoother shading, more realistic",
              "**Increases** (in proportion to the bits per pixel)"
            ],
            [
              "**Double** the width **and** the height",
              "—",
              "**4 ×** larger (4 × as many pixels)"
            ],
            [
              "Change colour depth from 8 to 24 bits",
              "—",
              "**3 ×** larger"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Calculating the size of a bitmap file",
      "objectives": [
        "11.1.1.6"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "definition",
          "term": "File size of a bitmap image",
          "content": "**File size (bits) = width (pixels) × height (pixels) × colour depth (bits per pixel)**\n\nThen convert: **÷ 8 → bytes**, **÷ 1024 → kilobytes (KB)**, **÷ 1024 → megabytes (MB)**. (Metadata and compression are ignored in these calculations.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "Units of storage",
          "columns": [
            "Unit",
            "Size"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1 byte",
              "8 bits"
            ],
            [
              "1 kilobyte (KB / KiB)",
              "1024 bytes"
            ],
            [
              "1 megabyte (MB / MiB)",
              "1024 KB = 1 048 576 bytes"
            ],
            [
              "1 gigabyte (GB / GiB)",
              "1024 MB"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "info",
          "title": "1000 or 1024?",
          "content": "In computer science, 1 KB is usually taken as **1024 bytes**. Some questions and manufacturers use **1000** (kB, MB in the SI system). Use the value the question gives; if it does not say, use **1024** and show your working so either method can be credited."
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked example 1 — 1024 × 768 pixels, 24-bit colour",
          "content": "1. Pixels: 1024 × 768 = **786 432**\n2. Bits: 786 432 × 24 = **18 874 368 bits**\n3. Bytes: 18 874 368 ÷ 8 = **2 359 296 bytes**\n4. KB: 2 359 296 ÷ 1024 = **2304 KB**\n5. MB: 2304 ÷ 1024 = **2.25 MB**"
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked example 2 — 800 × 600 pixels, 256 colours",
          "content": "256 colours → colour depth **8 bits** (2⁸ = 256).\n800 × 600 × 8 = 3 840 000 bits → ÷ 8 = **480 000 bytes** → ÷ 1024 = **468.75 KB**."
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked example 3 — working backwards",
          "content": "A 640 × 480 image has a file size of **600 KB**. What is its colour depth?\n600 KB × 1024 × 8 = 4 915 200 bits. 4 915 200 ÷ (640 × 480) = 4 915 200 ÷ 307 200 = **16 bits per pixel**."
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Worked example 4 — how many photos fit?",
          "content": "A camera takes 4000 × 3000 photos in 24-bit colour: 4000 × 3000 × 24 ÷ 8 = 36 000 000 bytes ≈ **34.3 MB** each. A 16 GB memory card (16 × 1024 MB = 16 384 MB) holds about 16 384 ÷ 34.3 ≈ **477 photos** (without compression)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
