    {
      "title": "Exam practice and summary",
      "objectives": [
        "11.1.1.5",
        "11.1.1.6"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Exam-style answers",
          "content": "**Q: Explain how a bitmap image is represented in terms of pixels, resolution and colour depth. [3]**\nThe image is made of a grid of **pixels**, each storing **one colour as a binary code** (1); the **resolution** is the number of pixels (width × height) — more pixels give more detail (1); the **colour depth** is the number of bits per pixel, which sets the **number of colours** (2ⁿ) (1).\n\n**Q: Calculate the file size in KB of a 1280 × 720 image with 24-bit colour. Show your working. [3]**\n1280 × 720 × 24 = 22 118 400 bits (1); ÷ 8 = 2 764 800 bytes (1); ÷ 1024 = **2700 KB** (1).\n\n**Q: Explain the effect on the file size of changing the colour depth from 8 to 16 bits. [2]**\nEach pixel needs **twice as many bits** (1), so the file size **doubles** (1)."
        },
        {
          "type": "warning",
          "content": "- Forgetting to **divide by 8** to change bits into bytes.\n- Using the number of **colours** (256) instead of the **colour depth** (8 bits) in the formula.\n- Mixing 1000 and 1024 in one calculation.\n- Saying higher resolution means \"bigger pixels\" — it means **more** pixels."
        },
        {
